# Lesson 4, new exercise: QJT8 opposite A32 at no-trump. Lead the queen, then the jack, from dummy; West's king is trapped and you lose the lead only once.
title: Keep leading from dummy
id: L04-15
type: exercise
lesson: 4
concepts: 4.2, 3.5
student: N
board: 69
dealer: N
vulnerable: NS

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  A84    A93    KQ52   A32
  E  QJT97  852    A86    54
  S  K52    K74    J43    QJT8
  W  63     QJT6   T97    K976

auction:
  N 1NT    = 15-17 points, balanced
     not *: 17 points and a balanced hand: what does the lesson open?
  E Pass
  S 3NT    = 10+ points: game
  W Pass
  N Pass
  E Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 1NT and partner raised to game. You are declarer, because you
  bid no-trump first, and so East will lead to the first trick.

planning:
  winners: 7-10
  spades: 2 = The ace and king.
  hearts: 2 = The ace and king.
  diamonds: 2-3 = Two once the ace is gone. A third if they split 3-3.
  clubs: 1-3 = The ace. Two more if West has the king and you lead the queen and jack from dummy; only one more if you have to lose to the king.
  remind: You need 9 tricks. Count the obvious winners in each suit: spades, hearts, diamonds, clubs.
  remind: East's spades will be a threat once your stoppers are gone. How many times can you afford to give up the lead?
play:
  1   E SQ  S S2  W S3  N SA
      note: You'll have to give up the lead once, to the ♦A. If you also lose a club to the king, the defense gets in twice: enough to knock out your spade stoppers and cash East's spades. So try to take the clubs without losing one: lead them from dummy. Win the ♠A in your hand, and keep dummy's king as a stopper.
  2   N H3  E H2  S HK  W H6
      note: Over to dummy with the ♥K, to lead clubs from there.
  3   S CQ  W C6  N C2*  E C4
      note: Lead the ♣Q from dummy.
      C2 key: Let the queen run. If West has the king, it wins, and you're still in dummy.
      C2 not CA: Did you mean to win your own queen? Let it run: if West has the king, the queen wins and you're still in dummy to lead again.
  4   S CJ  W C7  N C3*  E C5
      note: The queen won. Now the jack, the same way.
      C3 key: Let it run again.
      C3 not CA: Did you notice West may still have the king? Let the jack run.
  5   S CT  W CK  N CA  E H5
      note: Lead the ten. West covers with the king, and your ace wins. Three club tricks, without losing the lead.
  6   N DK  E DA  S D3  W D7
      note: Now force out the ♦A. That's the only time you'll give up the lead.
  7   E SJ  S SK  W S6  N S4
      note: East knocks out your last spade stopper, but East has no way back in.
  8   S DJ  W D9  N D2  E D6
      note: Cash the diamonds. Dummy's jack first, the high card from the short side.
  9   S D4  W DT  N DQ  E D8
      note: Over to your queen.
  10  N HA  E H8  S H4  W HT
      note: That's nine tricks.
  11  N D5  E S7  S C8  W C9
      note: Both opponents followed to three diamonds, so your last one wins too.
  12  N S8  E ST  S S5  W HJ
      note: Nothing left to win. Give up the rest.
  13  E S9  S H7  W HQ  N H9
      note: 10 tricks: 3NT made with an overtrick.

play recap:
  The plan: you could afford to lose the lead only once, to the ♦A. So you
  needed the clubs without losing to the king, which meant leading them from
  dummy, with West's king trapped.

  Leading the queen and then the jack kept you in dummy each time they won,
  so one entry was enough. You could lead honors because you held the cards
  in between: the queen, jack and ten together. If East had held the king, it
  would have won, and East would have knocked out your last spade stopper
  before you could set up the diamonds.
